            "A sudden choking episode followed by unilateral wheeze, asymmetric breath sounds, persistent cough, recurrent focal pneumonia or unexplained stridor should trigger concern for airway foreign body even if the child later looks well. A normal chest radiograph does not exclude a radiolucent object. Acute complete obstruction is an airway-rescue problem; stable partial obstruction is a controlled rigid-bronchoscopy problem, not a reason for repeated blind extraction attempts.",
            "Objects lodge according to size, shape and airway geometry; bronchi are common, but laryngeal/tracheal foreign bodies can cause rapidly fatal obstruction. Organic objects such as nuts can swell and generate intense inflammation. Chronic retained objects can cause granulation, bronchiectatic change and recurrent infection, making delayed removal more difficult than the initial event.",
            "History is often the highest-yield test. Inspiratory/expiratory or bilateral decubitus radiographs may show air trapping, atelectasis, mediastinal shift or a radiopaque object, but negative imaging cannot overrule a convincing choking history. CT may help selected stable equivocal cases but should not delay bronchoscopy when suspicion is high. Flexible bronchoscopy can be diagnostic in some settings, but rigid bronchoscopy remains the definitive pediatric removal platform because it secures a working airway and allows instrumentation.",
