            "Perform flexible laryngoscopy and document position, airway aperture, secretion handling and associated neurologic findings. Review recent neck/chest surgery and intubation history. If the cause is unexplained, image the vagal/RLN course appropriately rather than labeling it idiopathic. Laryngeal EMG can provide information about denervation/reinnervation and prognosis; direct laryngoscopy with arytenoid palpation is important when posterior glottic stenosis or joint fixation is suspected.",
            "Acute respiratory compromise requires airway stabilization, which may mean intubation or tracheostomy depending on anatomy and trajectory. When recovery is plausible after recent nerve injury, a reversible airway strategy may be preferable to immediate destructive glottic-widening surgery. Persistent bilateral neurogenic immobility can be managed with tracheostomy, posterior cordotomy/cordectomy, arytenoid procedures or selected reinnervation/pacing approaches, balancing airway against voice and aspiration. Treat a mechanical posterior glottic scar as a scar problem rather than a nerve problem.",
