            ["Posterior Glottic Stenosis / Arytenoid Fixation", "Posterior Glottic Stenosis"],
            "Posterior glottic stenosis is a cicatricial fixation process of the posterior commissure/interarytenoid region, most often after prolonged intubation injury. It can mimic bilateral vocal-fold paralysis because both folds appear immobile or poorly abducting. A history of difficult/prolonged intubation, posterior scar, impaired arytenoid excursion and preserved neural activity should move PGS above neurogenic paralysis in the differential.",
            "Pressure injury over the posterior glottis causes ulceration, granulation and scar that can progress from an interarytenoid band to fixation of one or both cricoarytenoid joints. The key anatomic distinction is mechanical tethering/fixation at the posterior commissure and joints versus denervation along the vagus/RLN pathway. Severity/classification reflects how extensively scar involves the posterior commissure and cricoarytenoid joints.",
            "Flexible laryngoscopy identifies posterior scar and reduced abduction but cannot always prove joint fixation. Direct laryngoscopy/bronchoscopy with palpation of each arytenoid is the decisive mechanical assessment; compare passive mobility side-to-side. Laryngeal EMG helps when neurogenic paralysis remains in the differential. Evaluate associated subglottic/tracheal stenosis in patients with prior prolonged intubation because multilevel injury changes reconstruction.",
